<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Entitlements for the hardened-runtime signature of dist/Rekounts.app.

  NOT YET USED BY ANY BUILD. Signing needs the owner's Apple Developer account
  ($99/yr) — docs/macos-packaging.md spells out the full procedure and what it
  costs. This file exists so that when the account arrives, the signing step is
  one command and not a research task, and so the list can be reviewed now,
  while the reasoning is fresh, rather than under release pressure.

  Reviewed against a from-source reading of what Rekounts loads. Nothing here
  has been exercised by an actual codesign run.

  Three entitlements, and no more. Every extra one is attack surface, is
  something a notarization reviewer has to accept, and would weaken the claim on
  the tin — a dictation app that asks for the address book has explaining to do.
  Deliberately ABSENT and worth being absent:

    * com.apple.security.app-sandbox — Rekounts cannot be sandboxed. The whole
      app is a global hotkey listener that synthesizes keystrokes into other
      applications; that is the opposite of a sandbox. (It is also not required
      outside the Mac App Store, which is not the distribution channel — see
      docs/macos-packaging.md.)
    * com.apple.security.network.* — the app makes exactly two outbound
      requests, both user-initiated (the one-time model download and the update
      check), and neither needs an entitlement outside the sandbox. Declaring
      network entitlements on an app whose selling point is "your audio never
      leaves this machine" would be actively misleading.
    * com.apple.security.automation.apple-events — the paste path posts CGEvents
      at the HID tap, not AppleScript. The consent it needs is Accessibility,
      which is a TCC grant the user makes in System Settings, not an
      entitlement.
    * com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit — CPython does not JIT, and neither
      ctranslate2 nor onnxruntime asks for a JIT mapping. The
      allow-unsigned-executable-memory below is the weaker one that is actually
      needed.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- CPython's ctypes trampolines and the native wheels' loaders write to
       pages they then execute. Without this the hardened runtime kills the
       process at import time, before any Rekounts code runs. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- PyInstaller ships the pyobjc, ctranslate2, onnxruntime, av and PySide6
       dylibs inside the bundle. Library validation would refuse to load any
       library not signed by the same Team ID, i.e. all of them. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- Declares microphone use. This is the entitlement half; the user-facing
       half is NSMicrophoneUsageDescription in Rekounts-macos.spec's info_plist,
       and macOS terminates the app on first mic open if that string is absent. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
